-- Demonstrates "assoclink" -- the one mechanism no other example in this
-- directory shows yet -- in a small, purely symbolic graph-theoretic
-- setting: an abstract, unordered graph refined into a concrete DAG.
--
--   M2 Node/Edge         -- an abstract directed graph: just vertices and
--                            edges, no acyclicity built in (only that an
--                            edge can't be a self-loop).
--   M1 OrderedNode/DirectedEdge -- a concrete instance: every node gets a
--                            topological "order" number, and its OWN,
--                            separately-declared association
--                            DirectedEdge -- linked back to M2's Edge via
--                            "assoclink", not inherited automatically the
--                            way an unchanged association would be.
--
-- Why declare a whole new association instead of just inheriting Edge
-- unchanged (the way MLM-USE/GraphColoring.use's AdjacentTo is inherited
-- by ColoredVertex with no assoclink at all)? Because here the roles
-- themselves change name (tail/head -> source/target) as part of the
-- refinement -- assoclink is what says "DirectedEdge at M1 is what Edge
-- at M2 becomes here", role by role:
--     assoclink DirectedEdge : Edge
--       tail -> source
--       head -> target
--     end
-- (Read as PARENT role -> CHILD role -- confirmed against the existing
-- ABCD test fixtures in use-core/src/test/resources/org/tzi/use/mlmParser/,
-- since the .gpart grammar's own variable names, childRoleName1/
-- parentRoleName1, actually name the two sides the other way around.)
--
-- The real payoff is Acyclic, in inter-constraints: it can ONLY be
-- stated at M1, because "order" -- the whole reason the constraint is
-- expressible at all -- doesn't exist until M1. This is the case for
-- levels existing in the first place: some invariants are simply not
-- statable in terms of the abstract vertices/edges alone, only once a
-- concrete refinement adds the extra structure (here, a numbering) that
-- makes them checkable.
--
-- Compare examples/CatMLM/DagRefinement.use, which shows the same domain
-- and runs into a hard wall trying to express either of these two
-- things.

MLM DagRefinement

model M2

class Node
end

association Edge between
  Node[*] role tail
  Node[*] role head
end

constraints
context Node inv NoSelfLoop:
  self.head->excludes(self)


model M1

class OrderedNode
attributes
  order: Integer
end

association DirectedEdge between
  OrderedNode[*] role source
  OrderedNode[*] role target
end


inter-constraints
context M1@OrderedNode inv Acyclic:
  self.target->forAll(t | t.order > self.order)


mediator M2 < NONE
end

mediator M1 < M2
clabject OrderedNode : Node
end

assoclink DirectedEdge : Edge
  tail -> source
  head -> target
end
end
